1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:01,240
Auto bridge in the world.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:05,820
Two parallel roadways spanning a lake 24 miles wide.

3
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:09,860
Made entirely of thousands of prefabricated parts.

4
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:14,680
Hidden in each span, the techno secret of the bridge's success.

5
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:19,880
An engineering breakthrough that made possible a record-setting triumph.

6
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,680
Now, the Lake Pontchartrain Causeway on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,120
Auto-

8
00:00:36,121 --> 00:00:48,980
In the land of voodoo and Mardi Gras,

9
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:50,920
Jambalaya and Zydeco

10
00:00:50,921 --> 00:00:52,780
is an engineering marvel

11
00:00:52,781 --> 00:00:54,380
that seems to go on forever.

12
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:56,260
Head toward the lake

13
00:00:56,261 --> 00:00:58,060
and drive on to a concrete span,

14
00:00:58,061 --> 00:01:01,960
one of two that crosses the largest inland body of water in Louisiana.

15
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:06,980
Speed along the highway, two lanes in each direction,

16
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:09,580
toward the north shore of Lake Pontchartrain,

17
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:12,580
and you'll lose sight of land in all directions.

18
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:16,080
That's because at the 12-mile mark,

19
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:19,340
you've only reached the halfway point

20
00:01:19,341 --> 00:01:22,800
on what is the longest overwater highway bridge in the world.

21
00:01:22,801 --> 00:01:29,800
The bridge actually traverses roughly one one-thousandth of the Earth's circumference.

22
00:01:29,801 --> 00:01:39,800
Whatever the causeway may lack in visual flamboyance, it makes up for in engineering and endurance.

23
00:01:39,801 --> 00:01:46,800
The single most important thing about this bridge is that it is 24 miles long,

24
00:01:46,801 --> 00:01:50,500
and it was built using manufacturing methods,

25
00:01:51,200 --> 00:02:01,280
where they built modular beams and piles and pile caps and slabs in a very mechanized way.

26
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:04,420
The beauty of the causeway is in the eye of the beholder.

27
00:02:04,421 --> 00:02:10,360
The parallel span stretching out to where you can see no land anywhere in sight

28
00:02:10,361 --> 00:02:16,540
is a beautiful vision with the background of one of the most beautiful sunsets in the world.

29
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:24,160
These two roadways are 84 feet apart.

30
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:31,000
On any given weekday, as many as 30,000 vehicles can pack onto these concrete trestles, made

31
00:02:31,001 --> 00:02:33,400
up of thousands of separate precast sections.

32
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:39,820
As cars speed across from New Orleans to the south and Mandeville to the north, large

33
00:02:39,821 --> 00:02:43,380
boats and barges pass under the bridge eight miles from the north shore.

34
00:02:43,381 --> 00:02:50,620
A bascule, or drawbridge, lifts four steel grid sections of road called leaves, nearly

35
00:02:50,621 --> 00:02:51,620
vertical.

36
00:02:51,621 --> 00:02:55,760
When we turn on the lights to the gates, you can't do this out of sequence.

37
00:02:55,761 --> 00:03:01,200
It has a fail-safe mechanism to keep it from just opening automatically.

38
00:03:01,201 --> 00:03:06,640
The gates lights have to come on, then the gates have to come down, and then we can release

39
00:03:06,641 --> 00:03:12,020
the emergency brakes here and mash the raise buttons.

40
00:03:12,021 --> 00:03:15,620
This is for the northbound spans.

41
00:03:15,621 --> 00:03:28,780
The procedure to raise the southbound leaves is identical.

42
00:03:28,781 --> 00:03:32,900
Smaller boats can pass beneath two matching raised sections of the causeway called marine

43
00:03:32,901 --> 00:03:37,220
spans, which provide 50 feet of vertical clearance.

44
00:03:37,221 --> 00:03:42,900
60 sets of smaller marine humps provide 25 feet of vertical clearance.

45
00:03:42,901 --> 00:03:47,880
Smaller pleasure boats simply pass under the roadways, which normally have between 15 and

46
00:03:47,881 --> 00:03:50,780
16 feet of vertical clearance.

47
00:03:50,781 --> 00:03:57,380
At its highest point, the bridge's roadway is never more than 60 feet above the water.

48
00:03:57,381 --> 00:04:02,520
Seven turnouts spaced along the spans provide a safe haven for drivers with breakdowns,

49
00:04:02,521 --> 00:04:04,720
for people who decide to go back the way they came.

50
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:10,840
But there are no breakdown lanes on the bridge,

51
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:13,600
although the Causeway's guardians can lend a helping hand.

52
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:17,380
Our maintenance department is responsible for three roadways,

53
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:22,020
or three orders that, you know, enter into the bridge going southbound.

54
00:04:22,021 --> 00:04:26,180
We assist motorists with flats, wrecks, accidents, things like that.

55
00:04:26,420 --> 00:04:28,880
We don't issue tickets. We're mostly there for assistance.

56
00:04:28,881 --> 00:04:32,380
Northbound commuters get a free ride, but the southbound

57
00:04:32,381 --> 00:04:36,880
lanes are hit with a $3 toll, which can be paid in cash or

58
00:04:36,881 --> 00:04:39,880
charged using a tagging system.

59
00:04:39,881 --> 00:04:42,880
Just don't try to blow by the toll booth on your way to the

60
00:04:42,881 --> 00:04:43,880
French Quarter.

61
00:04:43,881 --> 00:04:45,880
Yeah, they got cameras around, they got the police that

62
00:04:45,881 --> 00:04:46,880
monitors and stuff.

63
00:04:46,881 --> 00:04:49,880
Like if somebody was to run the lane, we would call them in on

64
00:04:49,881 --> 00:04:51,880
the radio and they would get them.

65
00:04:51,881 --> 00:04:54,880
There's no place to hide on a 24-mile concrete slab in the

66
00:04:54,881 --> 00:04:55,880
middle of a lake.

67
00:04:55,881 --> 00:04:58,280
mile concrete slab in the middle of the lake.

68
00:04:58,281 --> 00:05:02,480
Once you go out there eight miles, you can't see anything but water.

69
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:05,280
You're over the horizon.

70
00:05:05,281 --> 00:05:09,880
You can't see New Orleans anymore, and you're damn sure you can't see Mandeville at all.

71
00:05:09,881 --> 00:05:15,280
And for the next eight miles, you're completely surrounded by water, and you can't see anything

72
00:05:15,281 --> 00:05:18,080
by water because everything's over the horizon.

73
00:05:18,081 --> 00:05:25,780
Being surrounded by water on the causeway is a little like living in southern Louisiana.

74
00:05:25,781 --> 00:05:30,740
on the Mississippi Delta, a region that is nearly surrounded by huge bodies of water.

75
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:35,040
Consequently, bridges have always been a necessity around New Orleans.

76
00:05:37,780 --> 00:05:42,040
In one direction is the Gulf of Mexico, in another, the mighty Mississippi,

77
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:45,160
and in another, Brackish Lake Pontchartrain.

78
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:48,540
The lake is actually an estuary off the Gulf of Mexico,

79
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:51,760
with an average depth of between 10 and 16 feet.

80
00:05:51,761 --> 00:05:55,760
But at 610 square miles, it was always a significant travel

81
00:05:55,761 --> 00:05:58,760
barrier between the north and the south.

82
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:04,760
The land mass described by these water boundaries is known as

83
00:06:04,761 --> 00:06:08,760
the Isle of Orleans. Drainage had been a problem since the

84
00:06:08,761 --> 00:06:12,760
city was founded in 1718, well into the 19th century.

85
00:06:12,761 --> 00:06:15,760
You might think about New Orleans as half of an oyster

86
00:06:15,761 --> 00:06:20,760
shell. And along the river, the ground is higher because the

87
00:06:20,761 --> 00:06:24,320
higher because when the river overflowed every year,

88
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:26,940
there was a sedimentation process.

89
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:31,240
And the part that would dry up last as the floods receded

90
00:06:31,241 --> 00:06:32,860
was right next to the riverbank.

91
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:37,560
During the region's many torrential rains,

92
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:40,520
city dwellers have always escaped from low areas,

93
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:43,680
moving toward the river, or better still,

94
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:45,560
across the lake where the land is high,

95
00:06:46,220 --> 00:06:48,920
far from the lurking danger of stagnant, polluted water.

96
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:58,600
The ever-looming threat of a flood actually hastened the need for a bridge as an escape route during the city's past.

97
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:08,720
There was inadequate municipal sanitation, so there would be outbreaks of cholera, typhus, and other diseases.

98
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:17,640
So New Orleans was thought of as unhealthy, and if you could get out of the city in the summertime, that was the thing to do.

99
00:07:17,641 --> 00:07:20,640
And you could tell by reading the newspapers in June when they

100
00:07:20,740 --> 00:07:23,240
started talking about how healthy it was that something

101
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:26,540
terrible had broken out because they knew once everybody left

102
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:29,480
there wouldn't be any advertising.

103
00:07:29,580 --> 00:07:33,080
The place to go was and still is, across the lake to the

104
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:34,400
north shore.

105
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:37,400
Towns like Mandeville and Slidell and Madisonville were

106
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:41,400
known for their pine-scented fresh air and their spas.

107
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:45,240
But there were no quick and easy ways around the massive lake.

108
00:07:45,241 --> 00:07:50,780
Depending on the route and the conditions, an automobile journey might take four or five hours.

109
00:07:51,620 --> 00:07:57,320
Ferry service was inconsistent, expensive, and sometimes dangerous because of potential squalls on the lake.

110
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:03,460
Although some operators had periods of modest success, none of the ferries lasted.

111
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:08,500
Many people chose to stay put rather than brave the journey in the oppressive heat.

112
00:08:08,501 --> 00:08:15,100
But as the automobile revolutionized transportation in America, the people of Louisiana began

113
00:08:15,101 --> 00:08:22,600
to look upon Lake Pontchartrain as a hurdle to be leapt, rather than a wall to be flanked.

114
00:08:22,601 --> 00:08:27,540
During the flush years of the mid-1920s, dreamers in and around New Orleans began proposing

115
00:08:27,541 --> 00:08:33,260
schemes to bring automobile traffic to the north shore of Lake Pontchartrain.

116
00:08:33,261 --> 00:08:35,940
Some of these ideas were more ambitious than they were realistic.

117
00:08:35,941 --> 00:08:44,960
There was a proposal on the North Shore in the 1920s to build a causeway to New Orleans.

118
00:08:45,540 --> 00:08:51,100
And what they planned to do was to dredge a series of islands and then build bridges connecting the islands.

119
00:08:51,780 --> 00:08:58,800
And once they had the islands in place, they would subdivide them, sell the lots, and that would pay the cost to the project.

120
00:08:59,420 --> 00:09:01,600
Well, lo and behold, this was moving ahead.

121
00:09:01,601 --> 00:09:07,300
They'd sold stock, and then all of a sudden, two guys named Watson Williams came along,

122
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:13,320
and they said they were going to build a toll bridge at the other side of the lake.

123
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:18,980
Watson and Williams planned a seven-mile bridge across the eastern end of Lake Pontchartrain

124
00:09:18,981 --> 00:09:21,040
using a simple concrete structure.

125
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:27,280
But the effect of building this bridge was to knock the props out from under the people

126
00:09:27,281 --> 00:09:30,600
who were going to build the series of islands, and that collapsed.

127
00:09:30,601 --> 00:09:35,600
that the 25 or more million American families...

128
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:40,700
Populist Louisiana politician Huey Long opposed the concept of a toll bridge

129
00:09:40,701 --> 00:09:44,240
and campaigned vigorously against the Watson and Williams toll bridge

130
00:09:44,241 --> 00:09:47,480
during his unsuccessful run for governor in 1924.

131
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:50,960
When Long finally won the governorship four years later,

132
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:53,720
the Watson and Williams Bridge was open for business,

133
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:56,120
charging $1.25 per car.

134
00:09:57,080 --> 00:10:01,280
Long promptly built a free bridge just to the east of the Watson-Williams Bridge.

135
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:03,460
All these bridges still stand.

136
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:07,240
And incidentally, that put the Watson-Williams Bridge Company out of business.

137
00:10:07,660 --> 00:10:11,640
And the state bought the Watson-Williams Bridge for cents on a dollar.

138
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:18,820
The results of these and other bridges cutting shorter paths across the lake was a quicker commute.

139
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:22,880
Now the lake could be traversed in as little as an hour and a half.

140
00:10:22,881 --> 00:10:29,880
But none of these other bridges dared to cross straight down the middle of the lake, as the Bold Island Scheme had proposed.

141
00:10:29,881 --> 00:10:36,880
It was not until the mid-1950s that a new scheme for crossing Lake Pontchartrain would gain momentum.

142
00:10:36,881 --> 00:10:41,880
It was driven as much by new technology as by demand for a bridge.

143
00:10:41,881 --> 00:10:45,580
Bridge.

144
00:10:45,581 --> 00:10:51,200
More than 5,000 years ago, melting polar ice caps raised the sea level in the Gulf of Mexico,

145
00:10:51,201 --> 00:10:54,080
creating what is now Lake Pontchartrain.

146
00:10:54,081 --> 00:11:01,220
The Lake Pontchartrain Causeway will return on Modern Marvels.

147
00:11:01,221 --> 00:11:08,980
The story of the Lake Pontchartrain Bridge is the story of pre-stressed concrete technology.

148
00:11:08,981 --> 00:11:15,140
In the 1950s, Maxwell Upson of the Raymond Concrete Pile Company of New York began conducting

149
00:11:15,141 --> 00:11:17,740
research on pre-stressed concrete.

150
00:11:17,741 --> 00:11:21,920
Pre-stressed concrete was a new and largely untried construction method for such large

151
00:11:21,921 --> 00:11:28,360
projects, at least in the United States back in the late 1940s and early 1950s.

152
00:11:28,361 --> 00:11:35,860
Concrete, that ubiquitous mixture of cement, sand, gravel and water, by itself has structural

153
00:11:35,861 --> 00:11:36,860
weaknesses.

154
00:11:36,861 --> 00:11:43,040
If you were to make a, let's say a concrete plank, let's use that as an example.

155
00:11:43,540 --> 00:11:48,160
Let's say you made one at 20 foot long and you went to one end and you picked it up real quick,

156
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:50,320
it very likely would break in the middle.

157
00:11:51,460 --> 00:11:54,120
Because concrete has high compressive strength,

158
00:11:54,440 --> 00:11:57,760
it can withstand forces that push it together without fracturing easily.

159
00:11:59,720 --> 00:12:02,340
But because concrete has low tensile strength,

160
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:05,980
in other words, it can't easily withstand forces that pull it apart,

161
00:12:05,981 --> 00:12:07,231
it will crack.

162
00:12:07,232 --> 00:12:14,380
Pushing forces on the top have no effect on the plank, but pulling forces on the

163
00:12:14,381 --> 00:12:15,561
bottom crack it.

164
00:12:15,562 --> 00:12:20,800
To solve this problem, the construction industry relied on laying steel

165
00:12:20,801 --> 00:12:26,188
rods inside the concrete, creating what is known as reinforced concrete.

166
00:12:26,189 --> 00:12:27,760
Steel rods have great

167
00:12:27,761 --> 00:12:33,540
tensile strength so that when the hardened concrete plank is lifted, weight is shifted to the rods.

168
00:12:35,980 --> 00:12:38,980
Although some cracking of the concrete is inevitable,

169
00:12:38,981 --> 00:12:42,980
pre-stressing improves on this method by adding tension to the slab.

170
00:12:42,981 --> 00:12:44,980
I'll give you an analogy first.

171
00:12:44,981 --> 00:12:50,980
If you took a set of books and you put them cover to cover and try to lift them up,

172
00:12:50,981 --> 00:12:56,980
you couldn't lift them up unless you took your hands, put your hands at the ends,

173
00:12:56,981 --> 00:12:59,980
and pressed the books together.

174
00:12:59,981 --> 00:13:04,980
Well, that pressing is actually what happens in a pre-stressed concrete beam.

175
00:13:04,981 --> 00:13:11,980
Pre-stressed concrete uses very strong steel cables or wires stretched inside the form where the slab will be poured.

176
00:13:11,981 --> 00:13:17,980
You hold them at one end and you jack them under high tensile strength at the other end.

177
00:13:17,981 --> 00:13:19,760
Then you pour the concrete.

178
00:13:19,761 --> 00:13:23,980
After the concrete has set, you cut the wires loose at each end,

179
00:13:23,981 --> 00:13:27,980
and they put the whole beam into compression.

180
00:13:27,981 --> 00:13:33,980
Then you can pick this beam up and it never goes in tension on the bottom, never cracks.

181
00:13:33,981 --> 00:13:39,680
The first major pre-stressed concrete bridge was built in Philadelphia in 1949.

182
00:13:40,100 --> 00:13:41,480
It's the Walnut Street Bridge.

183
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:49,240
This bridge came along while pre-stressed concrete bridges were undergoing a lot of development.

184
00:13:51,140 --> 00:13:57,860
The Walnut Street Bridge proved to American engineers that pre-stressed concrete could be used for large public works projects.

185
00:13:58,600 --> 00:13:59,620
Other bridges would follow.

186
00:13:59,621 --> 00:14:08,480
I had a call from Maxwell Upson, and that was almost like getting a call from one of

187
00:14:08,481 --> 00:14:09,480
the disciples.

188
00:14:09,481 --> 00:14:15,500
He was one of a handful of the most highly regarded engineers in the construction industry.

189
00:14:15,501 --> 00:14:19,900
He had invented several different types of concrete piles used all over the country and

190
00:14:19,901 --> 00:14:21,620
the world.

191
00:14:21,621 --> 00:14:28,740
And this was his latest invention, a pre-stressed cylindrical concrete pile.

192
00:14:33,949 --> 00:14:37,311
The pile Upson invented was essentially concrete and steel.

193
00:14:37,312 --> 00:14:40,730
Upson applied the pre-stress technology to concrete pilings.

194
00:14:40,731 --> 00:14:47,967
Pilings are the round supports found on bridges and foundations that carry structural load and keep structures from collapsing.

195
00:14:47,968 --> 00:14:52,583
But they can only be prefabricated or precast so big before they become unwieldy.

196
00:14:52,584 --> 00:14:56,800
Before Upson successes, 24 inches in diameter had been roughly that limit.

197
00:14:56,801 --> 00:15:06,680
so Upson came along and he said look why don't I make a bigger pile make it round

198
00:15:06,681 --> 00:15:12,940
and make it hollow and then pre-stress it. Raymond Concrete Pile Company under

199
00:15:12,941 --> 00:15:17,620
Upson's direction adopted a revolutionary method called the Senviro

200
00:15:17,621 --> 00:15:22,560
process to produce hollow pre-stressed concrete piles with a circumference of

201
00:15:22,561 --> 00:15:23,576
of 54 inches.

202
00:15:23,577 --> 00:15:29,280
The Sen and Sen Viro stood for centrifugal force, which made the concrete

203
00:15:29,281 --> 00:15:33,400
spin to the outside wall of the piling form.

204
00:15:33,401 --> 00:15:37,520
Vi stood for violent vibration that compacts

205
00:15:37,521 --> 00:15:38,334
the concrete.

206
00:15:38,335 --> 00:15:42,840
And Ro stood for rollers that roll the concrete hard against the outside

207
00:15:42,841 --> 00:15:43,541
form.

208
00:15:44,800 --> 00:15:51,960
After the concrete is placed in the pile, then it's spun at a higher speed to compact

209
00:15:51,961 --> 00:15:58,960
the concrete and also to remove any excess water.

210
00:15:58,961 --> 00:16:01,374
The less water, the stronger the concrete.

211
00:16:01,375 --> 00:16:03,960
Upson's Sen viral process could make concrete

212
00:16:03,961 --> 00:16:07,960
that could withstand pressures up to 10,000 pounds per square inch.

213
00:16:07,961 --> 00:16:10,007
So Upson said, let's do this.

214
00:16:10,008 --> 00:16:13,960
Let's take a 16-foot long pile or section, and let's put

215
00:16:13,961 --> 00:16:22,480
longitudinal holes in the wall then when you want to make a pile you bring up

216
00:16:22,481 --> 00:16:28,840
several these sections you line them up on a rack so that the holes all line up

217
00:16:28,841 --> 00:16:36,640
then you run high-strength steel wire through those holes at the far end you

218
00:16:36,641 --> 00:16:43,200
clamp something to hold it and that will push against the pile and at your end

219
00:16:43,201 --> 00:16:47,120
And you put jacks to also push against the pile.

220
00:16:47,380 --> 00:16:50,000
And you start to work these jacks.

221
00:16:52,040 --> 00:16:53,520
And you compress the pile.

222
00:16:54,980 --> 00:16:58,500
After the other end of the pile is plugged, wires are cut.

223
00:17:00,360 --> 00:17:04,580
Grout is then fed under high pressure into the holes of these post-tension piles.

224
00:17:05,940 --> 00:17:07,900
Now the piling is pre-stressed.

225
00:17:09,500 --> 00:17:11,980
The seam is sealed where the piles fit together.

226
00:17:12,980 --> 00:17:16,060
Mud or silt fills the hollow piles when they're driven into the ground.

227
00:17:17,780 --> 00:17:23,400
Upson had invented a very efficient way to make strong, wide, and long pre-stressed concrete piles,

228
00:17:23,940 --> 00:17:25,660
good for resisting erosion in water.

229
00:17:26,460 --> 00:17:28,180
And there were two obvious applications.

230
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:32,820
One was for platforms in the relatively new offshore oil drilling industry.

231
00:17:33,180 --> 00:17:38,520
The other was for highway construction, which went into overdrive under the new Eisenhower administration.

232
00:17:41,980 --> 00:17:47,260
The 30-year-old dream of spanning Lake Pontchartrain suddenly seemed a real possibility.

233
00:17:48,120 --> 00:17:51,620
A consortium of business interests formed the Louisiana Bridge Company.

234
00:17:52,360 --> 00:17:57,020
Their goal was to win the bid from the New Orleans Expressway Commission for construction of the causeway.

235
00:17:57,940 --> 00:18:05,200
By 1954, the legislature created the Greater New Orleans Expressway Commission

236
00:18:05,201 --> 00:18:08,280
that was to operate the causeway,

237
00:18:08,281 --> 00:18:12,380
and it was that commission that actually issued the bonds.

238
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:17,860
It was a remarkably successful illustration of local political cooperation.

239
00:18:18,440 --> 00:18:24,020
I can remember when I was a kid and one of the officials was talking to my father

240
00:18:24,021 --> 00:18:28,740
and he said, if this thing works, we're going to make a lot of money

241
00:18:28,741 --> 00:18:31,400
and if it doesn't, we're going to lose our shirts.

242
00:18:32,840 --> 00:18:36,620
In 1955, the Louisiana Bridge Company began work

243
00:18:36,621 --> 00:18:39,160
on what would become the first Lake Pontchartrain bridge.

244
00:18:40,180 --> 00:18:43,000
The bridge consortium built a concrete casting facility

245
00:18:43,001 --> 00:18:44,080
right on the lake shore,

246
00:18:44,300 --> 00:18:46,980
where the span of the bridge would reach its northernmost point.

247
00:18:48,900 --> 00:18:53,760
They bought 40 acres on the shore of Lake Pontchartrain

248
00:18:53,761 --> 00:18:56,240
between Mandeville and Lewisburg.

249
00:18:57,240 --> 00:19:00,640
And then they dredged a 1,200-foot-long canal

250
00:19:00,641 --> 00:19:03,580
that was 151 feet wide.

251
00:19:03,581 --> 00:19:04,780
It was 12 feet deep.

252
00:19:04,781 --> 00:19:09,100
and they precast the sections on the land.

253
00:19:10,660 --> 00:19:14,660
The canal enabled boat and barge traffic to deliver raw materials

254
00:19:14,661 --> 00:19:18,400
and manufactured sections of the bridge right into and out of the plant.

255
00:19:19,400 --> 00:19:23,400
Much of what was produced at the plant would be too massive to move over land.

256
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:27,600
Owning a manufacturing plant right next to the site of the causeway

257
00:19:27,601 --> 00:19:30,260
allowed the Louisiana Bridge Company to build

258
00:19:30,261 --> 00:19:33,520
what was essentially the world's first prefabricated bridge.

259
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:41,640
The main difference between this bridge and other bridges is the manufacturing plant was built especially for this bridge.

260
00:19:41,860 --> 00:19:49,200
So it was laid out in a very optimal way to manufacture all the components of the bridge in the most efficient way.

261
00:19:50,260 --> 00:19:58,560
In addition to the pilings, the piling caps and the roadway slabs would also be made of concrete at the job site, then pieced together on the lake.

262
00:19:58,760 --> 00:20:00,480
And it was built like a big tinker toy.

263
00:20:00,481 --> 00:20:03,700
We would cast the piling, cast the caps and cast the slabs,

264
00:20:04,440 --> 00:20:06,840
take them out in the field and put them together.

265
00:20:08,340 --> 00:20:12,240
The first pilings were sunk on May 23, 1955,

266
00:20:13,380 --> 00:20:16,140
four months after manufacture began in the yard.

267
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:20,140
With the clock ticking on the 24-month construction contract,

268
00:20:20,720 --> 00:20:23,060
the company now had less than two years

269
00:20:23,061 --> 00:20:25,540
to complete the longest bridge ever built.

270
00:20:27,320 --> 00:20:30,260
The Lake Pontchartrain Causeway has become a key stop

271
00:20:30,261 --> 00:20:35,760
for more than 200,000 purple Martins on their annual migration from Canada to South America

272
00:20:35,761 --> 00:20:37,320
and back.

273
00:20:37,321 --> 00:20:40,920
The birds roost in the understructure of the bridge.

274
00:20:40,921 --> 00:20:46,100
The Lake Pontchartrain Causeway will return on Modern Marvels.

275
00:20:46,101 --> 00:20:52,820
Throughout 1955 and much of 1956, prefabricated concrete sections of the Lake Pontchartrain

276
00:20:52,821 --> 00:21:03,340
causeway were being poured, cured, barged, and installed by a team of engineers and workmen

277
00:21:03,341 --> 00:21:05,168
numbering over 200.

278
00:21:05,169 --> 00:21:11,420
The process began with the 54-foot-long concrete slabs, the caps,

279
00:21:11,920 --> 00:21:13,028
and the pilings.

280
00:21:13,029 --> 00:21:18,500
The slabs are first constructed in a bed in the manufacturing plant. The bed is

281
00:21:18,501 --> 00:21:24,500
actually a form for the concrete for the slab. The bed is able to accommodate

282
00:21:24,501 --> 00:21:32,120
eight slabs lengthwise and three slabs across. So at one time they were able to

283
00:21:32,121 --> 00:21:40,260
make 24 deck slabs. Reinforcing bar mats were placed in the slab bed and tendons

284
00:21:40,261 --> 00:21:45,260
on the mats were pretensioned. Then concrete was poured over the mats. After

285
00:21:45,261 --> 00:21:51,880
After the concrete was poured, it was finished on the top with a finishing machine, and then

286
00:21:51,881 --> 00:21:53,760
it was cured.

287
00:21:53,761 --> 00:21:55,380
It was actually steam cured.

288
00:21:55,381 --> 00:22:03,600
They moved enclosures over the slabs and introduced steam into the enclosures to cure the slab.

289
00:22:03,601 --> 00:22:07,940
Steam curing the concrete slab sped up the time it took for the concrete to set up or

290
00:22:07,941 --> 00:22:10,020
dry.

291
00:22:10,021 --> 00:22:14,020
Slabs that would have taken about a month to cure could be done in about a week.

292
00:22:14,021 --> 00:22:21,020
Then the steel tendons would be cut, and the slabs moved to a storage area for additional curing.

293
00:22:21,021 --> 00:22:27,020
There the slabs would sit for about two weeks, until they were transported out onto the lake.

294
00:22:27,021 --> 00:22:30,020
Then you had a pre-stress slab, and that's the way they were made.

295
00:22:30,021 --> 00:22:35,020
Mass produced, loaded on a barge, hauled out, set in the bridge.

296
00:22:35,021 --> 00:22:41,020
Pilings were paired along the path of the bridge where two sections of roadway slabs were joined together.

297
00:22:41,021 --> 00:22:46,860
Here at these junctures called bents, a prefabricated concrete cap would fit over

298
00:22:46,861 --> 00:22:52,960
the two pilings below and create a connection for the two slabs on top.

299
00:22:52,961 --> 00:22:58,120
The first step in the process was driving the 88-foot-long pilings roughly 70 feet through

300
00:22:58,121 --> 00:22:59,120
the Louisiana mud.

301
00:22:59,121 --> 00:23:08,120
Barge-mounted cranes would lift the piles, the pile cap, and the slab sections into their

302
00:23:08,121 --> 00:23:09,120
place.

303
00:23:09,121 --> 00:23:17,320
Piles, of course, were lifted up and then driven down through the water, through the bottom of the lake,

304
00:23:17,740 --> 00:23:22,060
using a steam pile driver, assisted by water jetting.

305
00:23:23,520 --> 00:23:28,580
Raymond had a history of designing and building their own highly specialized construction equipment.

306
00:23:29,300 --> 00:23:33,300
Raymond went to work with developing the pile driving hammers to be used.

307
00:23:33,301 --> 00:23:37,600
They would drive two piles for every bent,

308
00:23:38,420 --> 00:23:40,560
and they'd move ahead and drive two more piles

309
00:23:40,561 --> 00:23:42,040
and move ahead and drive two more piles.

310
00:23:46,680 --> 00:23:50,360
The pile drivers were never able to punch the piles into bedrock

311
00:23:50,361 --> 00:23:52,420
because there wasn't any to be found.

312
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:54,260
There is no rock around here.

313
00:23:54,460 --> 00:23:58,600
The only rock down here may be about 20,000 feet deep.

314
00:23:59,220 --> 00:24:01,020
You find it when you drill oil wells.

315
00:24:01,021 --> 00:24:06,020
The piles are sitting in a very, very hard sand strata.

316
00:24:06,021 --> 00:24:11,020
Sand is a very, very good foundation material, confined sand I might add.

317
00:24:11,021 --> 00:24:15,020
It's very dense, you know, and of course you gain friction on the sides of the pile,

318
00:24:15,021 --> 00:24:18,020
creates a tremendous amount of load-bearing capacity.

319
00:24:18,021 --> 00:24:23,020
The hollow cylinder 54-inch diameter piles had surprisingly thin walls,

320
00:24:23,021 --> 00:24:26,020
only 4-5-8 inches thick.

321
00:24:26,021 --> 00:24:32,260
But the concrete was so dense because of the senviro process that it was exceptionally

322
00:24:32,261 --> 00:24:38,180
strong, even in the brackish water of Lake Pontchartrain, an environment hostile to concrete.

323
00:24:40,180 --> 00:24:45,580
After the piles were driven and the tops sawed off to a consistent height, another team would

324
00:24:45,581 --> 00:24:49,520
fit the multi-ton caps onto them using a large crane.

325
00:24:49,521 --> 00:24:52,420
This was a delicate operation requiring

326
00:24:52,421 --> 00:24:55,674
man and machine to work together with precision.

327
00:24:55,675 --> 00:24:59,200
One mistake, and the crane could easily crush a man.

328
00:25:00,560 --> 00:25:05,380
After the caps were in place, the final piece of the puzzle was brought in and mounted on top.

329
00:25:05,980 --> 00:25:14,380
The big slab was set with an A-frame derrick, and at that time the slabs weighed about 200 tons.

330
00:25:16,300 --> 00:25:19,750
Metal shims were used to achieve a perfectly level roadway.

331
00:25:19,751 --> 00:25:21,680
The slabs were mounted onto steel

332
00:25:21,681 --> 00:25:23,271
posts in the caps.

333
00:25:23,272 --> 00:25:29,280
Each slab was like every other, each cap identical, every piling the

334
00:25:29,281 --> 00:25:36,844
same. Eight miles from each shore, the draw bridges offered even greater clearance.

335
00:25:36,845 --> 00:25:37,545
Here,

336
00:25:37,546 --> 00:25:39,367
the bridge sloped up elegantly.

337
00:25:39,368 --> 00:25:43,340
The bascules raised the leaves using a pendulum-like mechanism

338
00:25:43,341 --> 00:25:44,628
and a small engine.

339
00:25:44,629 --> 00:25:49,100
A weight counterbalanced the weight of the leaves so that when the

340
00:25:49,101 --> 00:25:55,020
engine supplied a small force to the counterweight, the leaves would raise as the weight sank.

341
00:25:55,021 --> 00:25:59,980
Eliminating that force brought the leaves back down again. Other than the bascules,

342
00:25:59,981 --> 00:26:05,900
a single turnaround utility road and the periodic rises, all the bridge components were uniform.

343
00:26:05,901 --> 00:26:10,540
There were no significant delays or disasters and the process went like clockwork.

344
00:26:11,100 --> 00:26:14,300
You could just every day go out there and look and they were further across the lake.

345
00:26:14,301 --> 00:26:23,860
In a sense it was prefab, but the prefabrication was something of an engineering marvel too.

346
00:26:24,620 --> 00:26:33,820
And so it was basically a very simple operation and they moved with remarkable speed.

347
00:26:34,440 --> 00:26:39,120
And they finished the project four months before they had expected to.

348
00:26:39,121 --> 00:26:51,400
After 14 months and $30,677,000 added to the $21 million for the purchase of the land, the Lake Pontchartrain Causeway was completed.

349
00:26:52,300 --> 00:26:56,500
It opened on August 30, 1956 to great public fanfare.

350
00:26:57,800 --> 00:27:07,420
The two-lane highway trestle bridge used 2,243 precast panels, each 33 feet wide and 56 feet long.

351
00:27:07,421 --> 00:27:12,420
It stretched for 23.86 miles across the middle of the lake.

352
00:27:12,421 --> 00:27:16,420
There was a ribbon cutting at the south end.

353
00:27:16,421 --> 00:27:28,420
And then there was a motorcade that was supposed to be single file across the causeway to the north end where there would be more ceremonies.

354
00:27:28,421 --> 00:27:34,420
When they got to the north end, there were so many cars that there was no way for them to get off.

355
00:27:34,421 --> 00:27:38,980
off so the cars were back way up on the causeway.

356
00:27:38,981 --> 00:27:43,920
From that very first traffic jam, demand for the bridge, which cost a dollar to cross,

357
00:27:43,921 --> 00:27:46,143
was immediate and overwhelming.

358
00:27:46,144 --> 00:27:50,160
New Orleans was now 29 miles and 40 minutes closer using

359
00:27:50,161 --> 00:27:53,627
the causeway. People could commute from their homes in St.

360
00:27:53,628 --> 00:27:55,600
Tammany Parish on the North Shore

361
00:27:55,601 --> 00:28:01,780
to their jobs in New Orleans. Housing grew. Traffic grew.

362
00:28:01,781 --> 00:28:10,960
It certainly gave industry and commerce a boost, and it also provided another evacuation route out of the city of New Orleans,

363
00:28:11,160 --> 00:28:16,260
which most people will tell you is most prone to hurricanes and is least prepared.

364
00:28:17,860 --> 00:28:24,500
Within just a few years, the Causeway was overwhelmed with an average usage of 3,000 cars per day.

365
00:28:25,560 --> 00:28:31,320
After examining its options, the Causeway Commission soon realized there was only one sensible solution.

366
00:28:31,780 --> 00:28:38,920
It needed to build another bridge across Lake Pontchartrain.

367
00:28:38,921 --> 00:28:43,660
Water spouts, a type of tornado that forms over bodies of water like Lake Pontchartrain,

368
00:28:43,661 --> 00:28:48,020
occasionally hit the causeway but haven't caused structural damage.

369
00:28:48,021 --> 00:28:55,020
The Lake Pontchartrain Causeway will return on Modern Marbles.

370
00:28:55,021 --> 00:28:59,380
The Lake Pontchartrain Causeway was such a success that within a few years it was nearing

371
00:28:59,381 --> 00:29:00,880
capacity.

372
00:29:00,881 --> 00:29:04,160
Although people considered the bridge a boon, it had its drawbacks.

373
00:29:04,760 --> 00:29:07,640
The two-lane highway provided no safe zone for breakdowns,

374
00:29:07,980 --> 00:29:10,320
and the narrow roadway could be hazardous and fog.

375
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:12,700
Head-on collisions were also a danger.

376
00:29:14,140 --> 00:29:17,620
Preparations for another bridge went swiftly during the mid-1960s.

377
00:29:18,540 --> 00:29:21,120
The bridge consortium's adjacent yard was still operating,

378
00:29:21,360 --> 00:29:25,320
having been kept busy with other local bridge assignments since the first span was completed.

379
00:29:26,680 --> 00:29:30,160
Further, engineers only needed to look at the full-scale model

380
00:29:30,161 --> 00:29:35,200
sitting out in the lake for a template and designing a second bridge

381
00:29:35,760 --> 00:29:40,480
a new span begun in september of 1967 would attempt to address some of the

382
00:29:40,481 --> 00:29:44,640
drawbacks on the first span but there was no question that the

383
00:29:44,641 --> 00:29:48,880
prefabricated construction method would be used again everything had been

384
00:29:48,881 --> 00:29:52,080
tested and tried and worked out the equipment was all there

385
00:29:52,081 --> 00:29:57,280
there was one major major improvement and if you ever get cocky about thinking

386
00:29:57,281 --> 00:29:59,380
and you've done something, don't,

387
00:29:59,480 --> 00:30:01,320
because somebody's going to come along and find a better way.

388
00:30:01,680 --> 00:30:04,920
Somebody, I don't know who, came up with the idea,

389
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:06,480
let's put three piles in a bin.

390
00:30:08,540 --> 00:30:11,420
Slabs, which on the first span had been 56 feet long,

391
00:30:11,660 --> 00:30:14,000
would be 84 feet long on the second span.

392
00:30:14,120 --> 00:30:18,440
So that you're not using any more piles in the total structure,

393
00:30:19,080 --> 00:30:21,760
and you haven't increased the cost, but you made it a lot stronger.

394
00:30:22,300 --> 00:30:25,480
Which meant that every third joint you matched up with the old bridge,

395
00:30:25,481 --> 00:30:29,640
and that was a big help in laying out the bridge in the field.

396
00:30:31,020 --> 00:30:32,540
And that was the purpose of it.

397
00:30:33,380 --> 00:30:37,680
The change in the size of the slabs actually made their manufacture more cost efficient.

398
00:30:38,560 --> 00:30:43,580
But because of the increase in size, the barges and the cranes used to place them on the bridge

399
00:30:43,581 --> 00:30:46,680
all had to be larger than the ones used on the first span.

400
00:30:46,681 --> 00:30:49,340
These bridge sections weigh almost 300 tons apiece.

401
00:30:50,040 --> 00:30:52,360
And the one way you can transport them, that's on a barge.

402
00:30:53,060 --> 00:30:54,960
So everything has to be done on the water.

403
00:30:54,961 --> 00:30:59,940
This kind of bridge can't be built on land because you can't truck something that weighs 300 tons down the street.

404
00:31:00,900 --> 00:31:05,680
And the cranes we had were very large Clyde Whirley cranes and big A-frame cranes.

405
00:31:06,320 --> 00:31:09,800
With the yard producing as many as 20 slabs in seven days,

406
00:31:10,400 --> 00:31:15,100
at times the workers out on the lake were able to lay down over 1,600 feet of bridge a week.

407
00:31:17,020 --> 00:31:20,280
Consequently, construction moved even faster than it did on the first span.

408
00:31:20,281 --> 00:31:27,680
The new pilings were manufactured with a slightly improved, thicker, five-inch wall over those in the first bridge.

409
00:31:28,340 --> 00:31:34,180
They used a little thicker wall to get a little more cover over the reinforcing bars

410
00:31:34,181 --> 00:31:42,080
so that they had better resistance against salt attacking the reinforcing bars.

411
00:31:42,460 --> 00:31:47,840
The quality of the steel and the concrete had advanced in the decade between the first and second bridge construction.

412
00:31:47,841 --> 00:31:54,260
A humping or bowing in the concrete caused by the release of stress in the pre-stressed slabs

413
00:31:54,261 --> 00:31:58,060
was partially responsible for a somewhat bumpy ride on the first span.

414
00:31:59,080 --> 00:32:01,580
Engineers factored that bowing into the newer slabs.

415
00:32:02,300 --> 00:32:08,600
We made a real good job of putting a little sag in that bridge and making it come out of flat.

416
00:32:08,880 --> 00:32:10,140
In fact, that's what I did.

417
00:32:10,141 --> 00:32:13,220
I used to calculate all those things when I was working with them,

418
00:32:13,240 --> 00:32:14,560
and I made all the drawings for it.

419
00:32:14,561 --> 00:32:21,380
With two spans, the engineers were able to create seven connecting alleys, or crossovers, at intervals along the roadway.

420
00:32:21,840 --> 00:32:26,940
They had anticipated that because the bridges were 84 feet between them.

421
00:32:28,680 --> 00:32:30,980
And one of those spans would just fit in there.

422
00:32:31,940 --> 00:32:36,240
But as smoothly as the second bridge went up, the process wasn't entirely without incident.

423
00:32:36,560 --> 00:32:42,260
I remember one time I was out there and the nets were so bad that we thought we were going to have to quit working.

424
00:32:42,261 --> 00:32:47,560
We had a storm that hit us one afternoon with 80-mile-an-hour winds.

425
00:32:48,280 --> 00:32:50,000
And we talk about hurricane-enforced winds.

426
00:32:50,340 --> 00:32:52,540
And we knocked down a little piece of the bridge in the process.

427
00:32:52,700 --> 00:32:53,960
Some of our barges got loose.

428
00:32:55,380 --> 00:32:57,780
Sometimes the incidents were more than aggravating.

429
00:32:58,160 --> 00:32:58,880
They were tragic.

430
00:33:04,160 --> 00:33:07,980
We were setting a cap on the piling one day.

431
00:33:07,981 --> 00:33:10,940
We would cast these caps upside down.

432
00:33:10,941 --> 00:33:18,020
And in that shape upside down, there was reinforcing sticking up that would eventually project down into the piling.

433
00:33:18,740 --> 00:33:21,700
And on the job, they would take cables and rotate these over.

434
00:33:23,160 --> 00:33:26,360
And one day, the cables slipped.

435
00:33:26,920 --> 00:33:28,960
They rigged it slightly wrong.

436
00:33:30,440 --> 00:33:35,120
And this one guy got both arms and both legs cut off.

437
00:33:35,121 --> 00:33:39,520
Despite various injuries and two deaths,

438
00:33:40,140 --> 00:33:44,100
construction of the second bridge was remarkably efficient and cost-effective.

439
00:33:44,580 --> 00:33:47,000
The total price tag came in at $26 million,

440
00:33:47,500 --> 00:33:49,860
which was $3 million less than the first span.

441
00:33:50,480 --> 00:33:55,260
The second bridge actually ended up taking a little longer than the first bridge.

442
00:33:55,360 --> 00:34:01,320
It took 25 months, but it was still several months earlier than the schedule for that bridge.

443
00:34:01,321 --> 00:34:06,940
They also did some work on the first bridge. They made a few changes.

444
00:34:07,740 --> 00:34:11,940
Both of the bascules on the old bridge were replaced to meet new navigation regulations.

445
00:34:12,600 --> 00:34:17,400
The northern bascule was raised even higher and the opening widened to 125 feet.

446
00:34:19,060 --> 00:34:22,700
A matching bascule was included in the second bridge at the same location.

447
00:34:23,920 --> 00:34:28,200
The southern bascule was removed entirely and replaced with a channel crossing

448
00:34:28,201 --> 00:34:31,300
that provided a 45-foot clearance for boats and barges.

449
00:34:31,900 --> 00:34:34,720
It also had a matching crossing in the second bridge.

450
00:34:36,300 --> 00:34:39,000
We had to finish the new bridge completely

451
00:34:39,001 --> 00:34:41,920
and then tear out part of the old bridge

452
00:34:41,921 --> 00:34:45,200
and raise it up and rebuild part of the old bridge.

453
00:34:45,680 --> 00:34:46,840
It was pretty tricky.

454
00:34:48,180 --> 00:34:49,720
On May 10, 1969,

455
00:34:50,840 --> 00:34:53,640
the new four-lane Lake Pasher train causeway opened,

456
00:34:54,360 --> 00:34:57,300
with the easternmost span sending two lanes of traffic north

457
00:34:57,301 --> 00:35:00,640
and the westernmost span sending two lanes of traffic south.

458
00:35:01,340 --> 00:35:04,880
There was one notable difference between the otherwise twin spans.

459
00:35:05,640 --> 00:35:11,960
The new span was 1 one-hundredth of a mile longer at 23.87 miles than the old one,

460
00:35:12,580 --> 00:35:14,720
making it the longest in the world.

461
00:35:15,500 --> 00:35:19,700
From the moment the second span opened, bridge traffic has steadily climbed.

462
00:35:20,400 --> 00:35:24,060
Soon the causeway simply won't be able to handle the projected increase in traffic.

463
00:35:24,061 --> 00:35:31,060
Not surprisingly, talk has already begun on what kind of bridge the third causeway should be.

464
00:35:34,060 --> 00:35:42,060
To compensate for the curvature of the earth, the Lake Pontchartrain Causeway was built roughly two inches longer than the distance it spans.

465
00:35:42,061 --> 00:35:47,060
The Lake Pontchartrain Causeway will return on Modern Marvels.

466
00:35:47,061 --> 00:36:00,740
With over 50 miles of roadway, a police force of 18 full-time officers, and thousands of

467
00:36:00,741 --> 00:36:07,440
commuters driving by every day, the Lake Pontchartrain Causeway in some ways resembles a small town.

468
00:36:08,900 --> 00:36:13,320
The 117-person staff that monitors the bridge has many responsibilities.

469
00:36:13,321 --> 00:36:18,420
It seems though tailgating and speeding is our biggest concern out here.

470
00:36:18,421 --> 00:36:23,080
We do our best with the traffic though and with the bridge not having any shoulders and

471
00:36:23,081 --> 00:36:28,000
during our peak hours hard for our units to get out and it becomes very hectic.

472
00:36:28,001 --> 00:36:34,420
A $6 million 15 sign message board system warns motorists of traffic conditions.

473
00:36:34,421 --> 00:36:39,140
Instantly the signs would then change and say there's a breakdown ahead, merge left

474
00:36:39,141 --> 00:36:41,120
or right, whatever the case may be.

475
00:36:41,121 --> 00:36:45,440
And we also have our own radio station and we're able to tell our motorists to tune to

476
00:36:45,441 --> 00:36:49,800
that station and we can give them safety information and instructions.

477
00:36:49,801 --> 00:36:54,440
102 call boxes line the bridge in both directions.

478
00:36:54,441 --> 00:36:58,480
These safety precautions have contributed to the causeway's admirable safety record.

479
00:36:58,481 --> 00:37:03,680
But accidents still occur, although not necessarily on top of the roadway.

480
00:37:03,681 --> 00:37:07,940
There are a number of things that can happen when a marine vessel strikes the causeway.

481
00:37:07,941 --> 00:37:09,360
It can strike the pylons.

482
00:37:09,361 --> 00:37:12,260
These pylons are made to hold a load on top of them.

483
00:37:12,261 --> 00:37:14,600
They're not made to take a side strike.

484
00:37:14,601 --> 00:37:21,860
So a barge of weight significantly could damage and dislodge a slab or two or more.

485
00:37:21,861 --> 00:37:27,360
It also could get under the bridge and in heavy wave action lift a span.

486
00:37:27,361 --> 00:37:32,380
It could get in between the two bridges and it could move for several miles impacting

487
00:37:32,381 --> 00:37:35,540
a number of pylons.

488
00:37:35,541 --> 00:37:40,340
Although rare, these collisions have occurred 16 times over the life of the two spans.

489
00:37:41,300 --> 00:37:46,920
A number of the 55 travelers killed since 1971 died when their cars drove off the bridge.

490
00:37:48,620 --> 00:37:53,060
The original engineers could not have practically designed the bridge to withstand these impacts.

491
00:37:53,860 --> 00:37:58,220
You're talking about a 300-ton barge going at two or three knots,

492
00:37:58,740 --> 00:38:01,060
and you want to stop it in six inches?

493
00:38:01,620 --> 00:38:02,560
Forget it, man.

494
00:38:04,160 --> 00:38:05,300
It's not going to happen.

495
00:38:05,540 --> 00:38:12,220
However, the bridge builders did anticipate such collisions and fitted the causeway with a number of spare parts.

496
00:38:12,560 --> 00:38:17,240
On both sides of the bridge, you'll see a piece of bridge sitting out there in the lake, all built and finished.

497
00:38:17,760 --> 00:38:20,880
And you'll see piling sitting out there, and you'll see pile caps sitting there,

498
00:38:20,940 --> 00:38:26,300
and you'll see rigging and big spreader bars that we use to pick the slabs up with, all sitting there.

499
00:38:26,301 --> 00:38:30,380
A contractor with big equipment can move in, and if they know what they're doing,

500
00:38:30,460 --> 00:38:32,980
they can have that bridge open back in a matter of days.

501
00:38:33,800 --> 00:38:37,360
But the causeway isn't just sitting around waiting to be struck by a barge.

502
00:38:37,361 --> 00:38:43,160
We have a radar system out there now that if you get within one mile of the causeway bridge,

503
00:38:43,220 --> 00:38:44,140
an alarm will go off.

504
00:38:44,320 --> 00:38:49,800
We're aware of it, and we can actually project a potential striking point on the causeway.

505
00:38:50,320 --> 00:38:54,520
Back on the asphalt, the causeway puts a priority on the safety of its passengers,

506
00:38:55,220 --> 00:38:58,680
using a fleet of vehicles, including a truck with a rescue basket

507
00:38:58,681 --> 00:39:01,020
for people who may have gone over the side into the lake.

508
00:39:01,021 --> 00:39:04,720
We like to say we're the world's longest and safest bridge.

509
00:39:04,800 --> 00:39:10,240
At this point, we're about to enter our third year without a fatality,

510
00:39:10,560 --> 00:39:16,920
and we had a wonderful stretch of no fatalities from 1986 to 1991.

511
00:39:17,760 --> 00:39:23,760
So we've been in the forefront in the bridge industry as far as safety is concerned.

512
00:39:24,580 --> 00:39:27,100
Despite the causeway's impressive safety record,

513
00:39:27,580 --> 00:39:29,780
thick winter fog can wreak havoc,

514
00:39:29,781 --> 00:39:32,660
and winds of 50 miles an hour will shut it down.

515
00:39:33,400 --> 00:39:35,700
A team of safety officers and maintenance vehicles

516
00:39:35,701 --> 00:39:38,580
can be deployed to convoy travelers through the fog

517
00:39:38,581 --> 00:39:41,240
or quickly drag them off the road after a breakdown.

518
00:39:42,420 --> 00:39:45,540
We keep up over 80 police units

519
00:39:45,541 --> 00:39:47,300
and over 30 maintenance trucks

520
00:39:47,301 --> 00:39:50,540
and some other small equipment such as tractors

521
00:39:50,541 --> 00:39:53,740
and some six-wheelers and different things

522
00:39:53,741 --> 00:39:55,400
we use on our grounds work.

523
00:39:55,980 --> 00:39:58,320
Well, we put quite a few miles on them.

524
00:39:59,780 --> 00:40:05,380
The crew also has a specialized vehicle for monitoring the day-to-day health of the bridge itself.

525
00:40:06,420 --> 00:40:15,000
Called an underbridge inspection unit, it's actually a highly specialized 35-ton crane with a 50-foot extendable boom.

526
00:40:16,860 --> 00:40:20,420
It'll get us anywhere on this 24-mile bridge that we need to go.

527
00:40:20,860 --> 00:40:24,100
From the tallest of the humps to the lowest area, it'll get us anywhere.

528
00:40:24,340 --> 00:40:25,840
It'll go from one side to the other.

529
00:40:25,841 --> 00:40:33,140
We have to do an inspection of the entire bridge, and myself and one other engineer will ride.

530
00:40:33,260 --> 00:40:38,980
We'll look every inch of the bridge, looking at the piling, caps, the girders, the spans,

531
00:40:39,060 --> 00:40:41,860
any part of it that they want to look at, they're inspecting.

532
00:40:41,920 --> 00:40:46,840
We have to do this once every year, which takes approximately anywhere from a month to a month and a half to do.

533
00:40:49,680 --> 00:40:53,840
The causeway is also undergoing a multimillion-dollar structural maintenance program

534
00:40:53,841 --> 00:40:59,440
program designed to prolong its existence with an emphasis on the 50-year-old pilings that make

535
00:40:59,441 --> 00:41:01,482
the spans an engineering marvel.

536
00:41:01,483 --> 00:41:05,120
The lake is brackish water and the brackish water attacks

537
00:41:05,121 --> 00:41:05,821
concrete.

538
00:41:05,822 --> 00:41:11,840
The sulfides in there attack the cement and you do get some erosion particularly at the

539
00:41:12,480 --> 00:41:13,412
wash line.

540
00:41:13,413 --> 00:41:20,960
We inspect those to see what repairs that need to be made such as hairline cracks

541
00:41:20,961 --> 00:41:25,960
that are repaired by epoxy repairs of those cracks.

542
00:41:26,960 --> 00:41:29,960
Some of the most severely damaged piling

543
00:41:30,560 --> 00:41:34,840
are totally encapsulated with a fiberglass collar.

544
00:41:35,780 --> 00:41:38,440
We typically have about three crews out there

545
00:41:38,441 --> 00:41:42,680
totaling about anywhere from 17 to 20 people.

546
00:41:45,320 --> 00:41:48,880
One crew will clean the marine growth off of the piling

547
00:41:48,881 --> 00:41:54,280
using anywhere from 6,000 to 10,000 PSI water blasting above and below water.

548
00:41:54,840 --> 00:41:56,880
The second crew, the way we've got it worked,

549
00:41:57,040 --> 00:42:01,300
sets a 1-8th-inch thick fiberglass jacket that stays in place.

550
00:42:01,840 --> 00:42:04,580
And then the third crew basically comes behind him

551
00:42:04,581 --> 00:42:09,940
and pumps an underwater epoxy grout into the fiberglass form that stays there.

552
00:42:10,380 --> 00:42:12,760
As crews work to maintain the existing spans,

553
00:42:13,240 --> 00:42:17,000
others wrestle with long-term plans to deal with the ever-increasing bridge traffic,

554
00:42:17,001 --> 00:42:20,000
which is projected to reach capacity by 2018.

555
00:42:22,100 --> 00:42:24,600
Despite discussions of such exotic alternatives

556
00:42:24,601 --> 00:42:26,380
as a magnetic levitation train,

557
00:42:27,140 --> 00:42:29,020
a third auto span seems inevitable.

558
00:42:29,800 --> 00:42:32,300
What we would do is build a third span,

559
00:42:32,680 --> 00:42:34,740
and it would have a shoulder on it.

560
00:42:34,800 --> 00:42:36,760
So you would have, on the new span,

561
00:42:37,060 --> 00:42:39,560
you would have two lanes and a safety lane.

562
00:42:40,120 --> 00:42:42,760
Then you would leave the middle lane alone.

563
00:42:42,761 --> 00:42:50,680
On the original 1956 bridge, the sidewall barrier would be removed and a shoulder would

564
00:42:50,681 --> 00:42:51,381
be added.

565
00:42:51,382 --> 00:42:56,140
This would provide two lanes of traffic on the newest span going one direction with a

566
00:42:56,141 --> 00:43:01,180
shoulder and two lanes of traffic on the oldest bridge going another direction with a shoulder.

567
00:43:02,260 --> 00:43:06,502
The middle 1969 span would have one lane and a shoulder lane.

568
00:43:06,503 --> 00:43:08,380
This bridge could alternate

569
00:43:08,381 --> 00:43:12,940
directions depending on usage. That would give us three safe

570
00:43:14,380 --> 00:43:17,660
traffic lanes coming south in the morning and the middle one

571
00:43:17,661 --> 00:43:21,580
would be reversed, three safe lanes in the afternoon peak hour

572
00:43:21,581 --> 00:43:23,980
traffic. More than likely, the new

573
00:43:23,981 --> 00:43:27,740
span will be similar in design to the earlier spans, just clean

574
00:43:27,741 --> 00:43:30,940
lines running parallel across the lake for 24 miles.

575
00:43:30,941 --> 00:43:34,380
I love to go across it. I love to drive on it.

576
00:43:34,381 --> 00:43:40,420
You know, I think about all the people that were there and, you know, the things we went

577
00:43:40,421 --> 00:43:42,980
through, the guys we worked with.

578
00:43:42,981 --> 00:43:46,300
But we had people.

579
00:43:46,301 --> 00:43:47,300
We had people in that.

580
00:43:47,301 --> 00:43:48,760
We had skilled people.

581
00:43:48,761 --> 00:43:50,060
We had good people, you know.

582
00:43:50,061 --> 00:43:52,060
We had real hard working guys.

583
00:43:52,061 --> 00:44:00,560
We had people that were a lot of fun to be with.

584
00:44:00,561 --> 00:44:05,520
Those hundreds of workers employing the new and largely untried technology of pre-stressed

585
00:44:05,521 --> 00:44:11,780
concrete made these deceptively simple bridges a reality.

586
00:44:11,781 --> 00:44:18,820
Their Lake Pontchartrain Causeway proves that simplicity can be style and that straight

587
00:44:18,821 --> 00:44:21,080
lines can be sublime.

